# kanshi — automatic per-profile output config on hotplug/dock (Wayland replacement
# for the X11 monitor-setup xrandr logic). kanshi applies the profile whose set of
# outputs exactly matches what's connected. Positions are in LOGICAL pixels (after
# scale). Apps WITHOUT wp-fractional-scale-v1 blur at fractional scales, because the
# compositor resamples their integer-scaled buffer -- wezterm is one (verified 2026-09-23:
# the protocol name is absent from its binary). Chrome is NOT one any more: a WAYLAND_DEBUG
# trace at scale 1.75 on 2026-09-25 shows it binding wp_fractional_scale_manager_v1,
# receiving preferred_scale(210) = 1.75, and setting a viewport source of 1598px for a
# 913-logical window (913 x 1.75 = 1598, i.e. pixel-exact, no resampling). No flag needed;
# --enable-features=WaylandFractionalScaleV1 changes nothing because it is already on.
# Workspace->output assignment lives in wm/sway-only.

# Home: ultrawide (ASUS VG34VQL3A) left at native 1x, laptop to its right at 2x.
profile home {
    output HDMI-A-1 enable mode 3440x1440 scale 1 position 0,0
    output eDP-1 enable mode 3200x2000@60Hz scale 2 position 3440,0
}

# Office: Samsung 32" 4K (DP-3) at 1.4. Comfortable terminal size AND crisp Chrome:
# at 1.4 Chromium renders at 2x buffer and the compositor downsamples (supersampled =
# sharp), whereas at 1.25 it rendered at 1x + upsampled (blurry). So 1.4 beats 1.25 on
# both size and sharpness here. Laptop at 2x to its right (3840/1.4 = 2742 logical wide).
profile office {
    output DP-3 enable mode 3840x2160 scale 1.4 position 0,0
    output eDP-1 enable mode 3200x2000@60Hz scale 2 position 2742,0
}

# Laptop only. Slightly smaller than docked (2): you sit closer to the laptop when
# it's the only screen. 1.75 is fractional, but per the header Chrome handles that
# natively now, so it should be crisp. If Chrome looks soft here, suspect a STALE
# instance rather than the scale: Chrome does not always re-rasterize when the output
# scale changes under it, so one started before a profile switch stays at the old
# rasterization until restarted. Bump to 2 if you want to rule scaling out entirely.
profile laptop {
    output eDP-1 enable mode 3200x2000@60Hz scale 1.75 position 0,0
}
